        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "c547c8c6-1693-54a9-a202-d926e3e875c1",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Bowdoin College, Brown University, WBOR, Colgate University, Hobart College\n- Places: Maine, Commonwealth of Massachusetts, Providence, Rhode Island, Brunswick, United States\n- People: Alva Kelley\n- Topics: university founding and chartering, college athletics, radio broadcasting\n\nFacts:\n- Bowdoin College was chartered in 1794 when Maine was still part of the Commonwealth of Massachusetts.\n- Brown University was founded in 1764 as the College in the English Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations.\n- Brown University is the seventh-oldest institution of higher education in the United States and one of the nine colonial colleges chartered before the American Revolution.\n- Brown University is a private Ivy League research university in Providence, Rhode Island, United States.\n- Alva Kelley served as head football coach at Brown University from 1951 to 1958, at Colgate University from 1959 to 1961, and at Hobart College from 1963 to 1970.\n- Alva Kelley compiled a career college football coaching record of 60–98–5.\n- WBOR (91.1 FM) is a radio station licensed to Bowdoin College in Brunswick, Maine, United States.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b00c7aa3-f36f-5b4c-b6b8-f8de0610a789",
        "text": "Page: Bowdoin College. Sentence: At the time Bowdoin was chartered in 1794, Maine was still a part of the Commonwealth of Massachusetts.\nPage: Brown University. Sentence: Brown University is a private Ivy League research university in Providence, Rhode Island, United States.\nPage: Brown University. Sentence: Founded in 1764 as the College in the English Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations, Brown is the seventh-oldest institution of higher education in the United States and one of the nine colonial colleges chartered before the American Revolution.\nPage: WBOR. Sentence: WBOR (91.1 FM) is a radio station licensed to Bowdoin College in Brunswick, Maine, United States.\nPage: Alva Kelley. Sentence: He served as the head football coach at Brown University (1951–1958), Colgate University (1959–1961), and Hobart College (1963–1970), compiling a career college football record of 60–98–5.",
